-- title:
--   Lower bound $\alpha\ge K_c/(N_c+K_c-1+M_c)$
-- statement:
--   Let $q^s$ be feasible for Problem (2), $\pi$ a stationary distribution of the static chain concentrated on the allocation $(K_c)$, and $i$ a location of network $c$. Then
--
--   $$
--   \frac{K_c}{N_c+K_c-1+M_c}\le\alpha_i(\pi).
--   $$
--
--   On p. 18 the paper states, for $K\ge K_i>0$, $\frac{p_iK_i}{N_i+K_i-1+M_i(1-\alpha(q^s,K_i-1,N_i))}\ge\frac{p_iK_i}{N_i+K_i-1+M_i}$; by the recursion (5) the left side is $p_i\,\alpha(q^s,K_i,N_i)$. The same step ("Substituting $\alpha(K-1)$ with its lowest possible value which is $0$") closes the proof of Theorem 1 on p. 8. It turns the reward of the static policy into an explicit lower bound per network.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: trip durations i.i.d. with a general distribution with rational Laplace transform and mean 1/μij, represented by a network of exponential stages (p. 4) → a phase-type law per pair: finitely many stages, entry probabilities β (total ≤ 1; the defect is a zero-length trip), rates r between stages, exit rates e, an exit reachable from every stage; 1/μij = β(−T)⁻¹𝟙; reason: the paper's state counts vehicles at each stage of the transit queue (footnote 1), so the model exists exactly for laws with such a stage network, i.e. phase-type laws; the general (not only Coxian) form is taken
--   - encoding: demand, prices, rewards and travel data on the pairs (i,j) ∈ 𝒜 (Λij > 0) → all of them given on every pair of locations; reason: pairs with Λij = 0 enter every rate, sum and constraint with factor 0; no instance changes
--   - encoding: prices pij with acceptance fractions qij = 1 − Fij(pij) (p. 5) → the fractions q ∈ [0,1] and the per-trip rewards Iij(q) as primitives, q·Iij(q) concave on [0,1]; reason: the paper reformulates the policy in terms of the quantiles q (p. 5); concavity of q·I is its only requirement
--   - reading: piKi/(Ni+Ki−1+Mi(1−α(q^s,Ki−1,Ni))) ≥ piKi/(Ni+Ki−1+Mi) → Kc/(Nc+Kc−1+Mc) ≤ avail π i; reason: by (5) the left fraction is p_i·α(q^s,K_i,N_i), so the paper's inequality is this bound on α multiplied by p_i ≥ 0, which is how p. 8 uses it
--   - generalization: K ≥ Ki > 0 → any Kc ≥ 0; reason: at Kc = 0 the bound reads 0 ≤ α, also true
--   - reading: networks with N_i locations and workload M_i → connected components of the flow graph of q^s; Nc its number of locations; Mc summed over the pairs (i,j) with i in c; reason: Reading 4; pairs without flow contribute 0
--   - generalization: q^s optimal for Problem (2) with Δ ≥ K → q^s feasible for Problem (2), any Δ; reason: only the bounds 0 ≤ q^s ≤ 1 and balanced flows are used; an optimal q^s is feasible
--   - generalization: q^s induces one strongly connected network (p. 6; Section 3) → q^s induces any number of networks; the statement is per network; reason: Section 6 relaxes the assumption; a single network is the case of one component
--   - generalization: the network generated by 𝒩 and 𝒜 is strongly connected (p. 4, without loss of generality) → no connectivity assumption on the demand graph; reason: no step uses it; strongly connected instances are a special case
-- source:
--   Benjaafar, Shen, Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks, SSRN 3998297 (version of 2023-01-10), https://ssrn.com/abstract=3998297, proof of Theorem 4, case C = 2, p. 18; p. 8

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_IsFeasible2
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_staticPolicy
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_IsStationary
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_ConcentratedOn
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_avail
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_Nc
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_Mc

namespace VehShare.MultiNet

/-- **Lower bound on availability.**
Benjaafar, Shen, *Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks*, SSRN 3998297
(version of 2023-01-10), proof of Theorem 4, case `C = 2`, p. 18: "for any `K_i ∈ ℕ`, `K ≥ K_i > 0`,
`p_iK_i/(N_i+K_i−1+M_i(1−α(q^s,K_i−1,N_i))) ≥ p_iK_i/(N_i+K_i−1+M_i)`"; also p. 8 ("Substituting
`α(K − 1)` with its lowest possible value which is 0").

Let `q^s` be feasible for Problem (2). Under the static policy, for every stationary distribution `π`
concentrated on allocation `(K_c)` and every location `i` of network `c`:
`K_c / (N_c + K_c − 1 + M_c) ≤ α_i(π)`.

**Formalization Note.** When `K_c = 0` the left side is `0` (if also `N_c = 1` and `M_c = 0`, Lean's
`0 / 0 = 0`, the paper's value). Every quantity is cast to `ℝ` before `− 1`. Any `Δ`. -/
theorem availability_lower_bound (P : Instance) (K : ℕ) (Δ : ℝ) (qs : P.L → P.L → ℝ)
    (hqs : IsFeasible2 P Δ qs) (Kc : (flowGraph P qs).ConnectedComponent → ℕ)
    (π : State P K → ℝ) (hπ : IsStationary (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1) π)
    (hπc : ConcentratedOn P qs Kc π)
    (c : (flowGraph P qs).ConnectedComponent) (i : P.L)
    (hi : (flowGraph P qs).connectedComponentMk i = c) :
    (Kc c : ℝ) / ((Nc P qs c : ℝ) + (Kc c : ℝ) - 1 + Mc P qs c) ≤ avail π i := by sorry

end VehShare.MultiNet
